  heroImageTitle="LG Dishwasher Error Code Repair"
/>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Answer first</p>
    <h2>Before you book anything: CL isn't an error, and LE isn't CL.</h2>

    <p><strong><span class="code">CL</span> on an LG dishwasher is Control Lock (child lock) — a feature, cleared by holding the lock button about 3 seconds. It is not a fault. Don't confuse it with <span class="code">LE</span>, which is a real wash-motor error. For the actual faults, LG's core codes are readable once you know the pattern: <span class="code">OE</span> is drain (out), <span class="code">IE</span> is inlet (in), <span class="code">HE</span> is heater, <span class="code">AE</span> (= E1) is a leak, and <span class="code">FE</span> is overfill. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p>Here's the honest read. Most LG dishwasher faults land in a few buckets: a drain fault (OE), an inlet fault (IE), heat faults (HE, tE), motor faults (CE, LE, nE), a leak (AE/E1), and overfill (FE). A handful — CL, PF, bE — aren't hardware failures at all. Below we give you the full table, split into what you can clear yourself and what needs a tech.</p>

    <p>Two housekeeping notes: this is the <em>dishwasher</em> page — LG uses some of the same letters on its washers and refrigerators, so if you landed here for those, see <a href="/services/washer-repair/lg-error-codes/">LG washer error codes</a> or <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-error-codes/">LG refrigerator error codes</a>. Parent reference: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/error-codes/">all dishwasher error codes</a>. Brand pillar: <a href="/brands/lg-dishwasher-repair/">LG dishwasher repair</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Not-a-fault codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">CL, PF, bE — clear these yourself.</h2>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Display</th><th>What it is</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>CL</td><td>Control Lock / child lock (feature)</td><td>Hold the lock button ~3 seconds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>PF</td><td>Power failure mid-cycle</td><td>Restart the cycle after power returns</td></tr>
        <tr><td>bE</td><td>Over-sudsing (too much / wrong detergent, or not level)</td><td>Cut detergent, use dishwasher soap, level the unit</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><strong>LE</strong> looks similar to CL but is a real wash-motor fault — see the motor section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Full code table</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG dishwasher codes.</h2>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Common cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>OE</td><td>Drain (outlet) error</td><td>Filter clog, hose, air gap / disposal plug, drain pump</td></tr>
        <tr><td>IE</td><td>Inlet error (not enough water)</td><td>Supply valve, hose, inlet screen, inlet valve</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FE</td><td>Fill error (overfill / too much water)</td><td>Inlet valve stuck, float; drain pump auto-runs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AE / E1</td><td>Leak / overfill (float tripped)</td><td>Door gasket, hose, or sump — leak source (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HE</td><td>Heater error</td><td>Heating element or control (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>tE</td><td>Thermal / temperature-sensor error</td><td>Thermistor or overheat (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CE / LE</td><td>Motor / harness error</td><td>Wash motor or motor-to-board wiring (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>nE</td><td>Spray-arm (Vario) motor error</td><td>Spray-arm / impeller motor (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>PE</td><td>Water-level pressure sensor (older models)</td><td>Pressure sensor (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>bE</td><td>Over-sudsing</td><td>Detergent amount / type; unit not level (customer)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F31 / F35 / F37</td><td>Dynamic Heat Dry codes</td><td>Heat-dry fan / temp sensor / auto-open door (technician)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">Not shown here on purpose: dE and tCL. Those aren't valid LG <em>dishwasher</em> codes (dE appears on other LG appliance lines; tCL is an LG washer tub-clean reminder). If a chart lists them as LG dishwasher codes, it's mislabeled.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Drain &amp; inlet</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">OE is out, IE is in.</h2>
    <p><strong>OE (drain):</strong> the tub didn't empty. Clean the filter cylinder at the tub bottom, check the drain hose for kinks, and — a big one on new installs — make sure the garbage-disposal knockout plug was removed and the air gap is clear. If OE persists, the drain pump is next. The full drain walkthrough is on our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-draining/">dishwasher not draining</a> page.</p>
    <p><strong>IE (inlet):</strong> not enough water arrived. Supply valve fully open, fill hose not kinked, inlet screen clear of sediment. If the water's fine but IE stays, the inlet valve or level sensor is the suspect. Inlet valve runs $200 to $340. The letter is your clue — I for in, O for out.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Leak, heat &amp; motor</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">AE/E1, HE/tE, CE/LE/nE — the technician codes.</h2>
    <p><strong>AE / E1 (leak):</strong> water in the base pan tripped the float. Won't clear until the leak source (gasket, hose, sump) is found and fixed. Cost swings $200 to $980 by source — an on-site trace, not a reset. See <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/leaking-water/">dishwasher leaking water</a>.</p>
    <p><strong>HE / tE (heat):</strong> HE is a heater fault (can't heat, or water over ~149°F); tE is a temperature-sensor fault (water over ~194°F or a bad thermistor). Both need a meter — $200 to $460.</p>
    <p><strong>CE / LE / nE (motor):</strong> CE and LE are wash-motor or motor-harness faults; nE is the spray-arm (Vario) motor. Technician diagnosis, $300 to $820 depending on which motor and whether it's the harness or board. LE is the one people mistake for CL — it's real.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Heat-dry codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">F31, F35, F37 — Dynamic Heat Dry models.</h2>
    <p>If your LG has the Dynamic Heat Dry system and shows an F-code: F31 is a heat-dry fan-motor failure, F35 means the heat-dry temperature can't be measured (sensor), and F37 is an auto-open door mechanism error. All three are drying-hardware faults — technician-level, no customer reset. If dishes are also coming out wet, our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-drying/">dishwasher not drying</a> page covers the full drying picture, and these codes send us straight to the fan, sensor, or door actuator.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">DIY resets</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before you book a visit.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Clear Control Lock (CL).</strong> Hold the lock button ~3 seconds. CL isn't a fault.</li>
      <li><strong>Power cycle.</strong> Unplug 60 seconds (or flip the breaker), restore power, run a short cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean the filter (OE).</strong> Twist out the filter cylinder at the tub bottom, rinse, reinstall.</li>
      <li><strong>Check drain path (OE).</strong> Hose kinks, air gap, disposal knockout plug on new installs.</li>
      <li><strong>Check supply (IE).</strong> Valve open, hose not kinked, inlet screen clear.</li>
      <li><strong>Fix suds (bE).</strong> Cut detergent, use dishwasher soap only, confirm the unit is level.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Leak (AE/E1), heat (HE/tE), motor (CE/LE/nE), sensor (PE), and heat-dry (F31/F35/F37) codes don't clear from a customer reset when the hardware's down — those are worth a call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A young LG with one code is worth fixing; an old one stacking codes is a conversation.</h2>
    <p>A single drain, inlet, or leak fault on an LG dishwasher in its first several years is almost always a straightforward repair — bounded parts, clear cost. Once a machine is older and starts throwing several different codes across a short span, that's often the control board sending false signals, and we give you the honest repair-versus-replace math at the door. We'd rather tell you a tired unit isn't worth a board than sell you one. $89 diagnostic, waived if you go ahead with the repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Pricing</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Code-driven part costs.</h2>
    <p class="lead">$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. No emergency surcharge — same $89 anytime.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code-driven repair</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Diagnostic</td><td>$89, waived with repair</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drain clog / pump (OE)</td><td>$89 to $440</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inlet valve (IE / FE)</td><td>$200 to $340</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Leak repair (AE / E1)</td><td>$200 to $980 (by source)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heater / thermistor (HE / tE)</td><td>$200 to $460</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Motor / harness (CE / LE / nE)</td><td>$300 to $820</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water-level sensor (PE)</td><td>$200 to $380</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heat-dry fan / sensor / actuator (F31/F35/F37)</td><td>$260 to $620</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warranty</td><td>90 days parts and labor</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG dishwasher code questions.</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
